# Files Fern must not modify or delete when regenerating this repo.
#
# Fern owns the SDK source and its ci.yml, and prunes anything it did not generate — it
# reads this file from the pull request's base branch, so an entry only protects a file
# once it is on main.
#
# publish-main.yml is hand-written: it releases the whop_sdk crate when a merge to main
# lands a Cargo.toml [package] version that is not yet on crates.io. It is meant to be the
# only path to crates.io, and it authenticates with the CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN secret.
#
# Unlike the Ruby generator, the Fern Rust generator's ci.yml carries no publish job — it
# is check / compile / test on push and holds no id-token or registry credential — so
# there is no second path to the registry to neutralise, and ci.yml is left to Fern.
#
# LICENSE is hand-written and this entry keeps the next regeneration from deleting it.
# Fern does not carry over a LICENSE of its own for Rust, and the file ships inside the
# crate, so the licence Cargo.toml claims would otherwise be a claim with nothing behind
# it. It currently reads MIT because the Rust generator hardcodes `license = "MIT"` into
# Cargo.toml; whoever changes that has to change this file in the same commit, and the
# release workflow fails the two apart.
#
# src/helpers holds the hand-written helpers in this SDK. verify_webhook is the Standard
# Webhooks signature verifier: it restores the verification half of `client.webhooks.unwrap`,
# which the Stainless SDKs shipped and Fern cannot generate, because Fern generates from
# OpenAPI paths and `unwrap` was never a path. It depends only on base64/hmac/sha2 and the
# standard library, never on generated client code, so it survives the client being replaced.
#
# src/lib.rs is protected WITH it, and that entry is the load-bearing one. Rust compiles no
# file that no `mod` declares, and src/lib.rs is generated (`generateLibFile` in the
# generator bundle) — so without this entry a regeneration drops `pub mod helpers;` and
# src/helpers becomes a directory the crate does not contain. The Ruby generator has
# `requirePaths` for exactly this and the Rust generator has no equivalent: its whole config
# schema is crate/package metadata, extraDependencies, features and casing, with no key that
# injects a module. Owning the file is the only mechanism it offers.
#
# The cost of owning it is that the generator's own changes to lib.rs stop arriving. That
# file is 40 lines and nearly static — a crate-level doc comment whose Getting Started
# snippet is built from one endpoint, a module list of which only `environment` is
# conditional, and the re-exports. A local generation at 0.46.0 reproduces the tree's copy
# byte for byte apart from the two lines added here, so whoever bumps the generator should
# diff `.generated/rust-sdk/src/lib.rs` against this one and reapply.
#
# Nothing of this is enforced by a hand-written check, because it does not need to be: Fern's
# own ci.yml runs cargo check / build / test, tests/verify_webhook.rs reaches the helper
# through `whop_sdk::helpers`, and dropping either the module declaration or the crypto
# dependencies is a compile error rather than a silent regression. That is the Rust
# difference from Ruby, whose missing `require` had to be asserted for.
#
# The dependencies themselves live in Cargo.toml, which is generated, so they are restored
# from `extraDependencies` in the rust config in whop-monorepo's sdks/fern/generators.yml —
# `base64: "0.22"`, `hmac: "0.12"`, `sha2: "0.10"`. They and this file travel together.
#
# The helper's tests live inside src/helpers/verify_webhook.rs rather than in tests/, and
# that is a CI constraint, not a style choice: an integration test is a second binary linked
# against the whole crate, and `cargo test` on 82 resource clients and ~4k generated types
# was killed on a GitHub runner with the extra target and passes without it. An inline
# `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` adds no target at all, and it is protected by the src/helpers
# entry above. Nothing in it computes a signature with the code under test: every fixture is
# signed the way WebhooksManager::SignWebhook signs, which is the only way to catch the
# mismatch the helper exists to avoid — the backend HMACs with the literal bytes of the
# `ws_…` secret, and every Standard Webhooks library base64-decodes its key instead.
.github/workflows/publish-main.yml
LICENSE
src/helpers
src/lib.rs
